// Wall clocks, timelines, and the conversion between them.
//
// The axis this owns is **a conversion that can succeed twice**. Every other program in the set
// converts things that either work or do not, and `Option` and `Result` are the two shapes that
// covers. A wall clock reading turned into a moment is neither: it usually has one answer, in one
// hour of the year it has none, and in one other hour it has two — and both of the two are correct,
// for different questions. Nothing in the set had asked for that before.
//
// The program is a recurring-meeting expander, because that is where the distinction cannot be
// avoided. A meeting is a rule about a wall clock — every Tuesday at half past nine, in New York —
// and the answer a participant elsewhere needs is a point on the timeline. Expanding the rule puts
// the conversion in the middle of the program rather than in a corner of it.
//
// **The assertion the whole thing is for**: across a daylight-saving change, "next week's meeting"
// and "one hundred and sixty-eight hours from now" are different moments, and *both are right*.
// One is 167 hours later and reads as half past nine; the other is 168 hours later and reads as
// half past ten. A single type for both could only ever have been wrong about one of them.
//
// Every expected value came from outside: the transitions, the offsets and each meeting time were
// computed with Python's `zoneinfo` against the real tz database. That was worth doing for its own
// sake — Python subtracts two aware date-times in one zone by *wall clock*, so the first attempt at
// the reference values reported the week across the spring change as 168 hours. The confusion this
// program is about is not a hypothetical one.
//
// What it found, in the order it bit:
//
//   * **The difference of two moments cannot be spelled `-`.** Every operator row is
//     `op(self, rhs: Rhs) -> Self`, so an implementation says what it *takes* and never what it
//     *produces*. `Instant + Duration`, `Instant - Duration` and `Duration ± Duration` all write
//     themselves; `Instant - Instant -> Duration` is refused, because `Sub`'s result is fixed to
//     the type on the left. Three quarters of an algebra is spellable and the quarter that is not
//     is the operation the library exists to provide, so it is a named function called `since`.
//     Dispatching on the pair does not rescue it: the two implementations would differ in what
//     they produce, which no row can say.
//   * **A derived scalar and a one-field struct are exactly complementary, and neither is what a
//     quantity wants.** `type Instant = new i64` inherits its base's whole catalog for free — `==`,
//     `<`, `+`, `str` — and may replace none of it, so it arrives with `Instant + Instant`, which
//     is nonsense, and cannot be given `Instant + Duration`, which is not. A struct of one field
//     gets the algebra exactly right and arrives with nothing at all: five `impl` blocks per type
//     before anything can be compared or printed. So a quantity type chooses between the catalog
//     and the meaning, and this program pays the struct's price on every one of its six types.
//   * **The three-way answer needed nothing new**, and that is a result rather than an absence. A
//     data enum already spells "one answer, or none, or two, and here they are"; `Resolution` is an
//     ordinary enum and the compiler's coverage checking is what makes a caller handle all three.
//     The axis the problem was chosen for turns out to be covered by the machinery that was already
//     there — which is the useful thing to know before designing a library around it.
//   * **An enum still cannot render its own variant names**, third program to report it. `ZoneId`,
//     `Weekday` and `Resolution` each carry a hand-written map from variant to word, and the two
//     that are simple enums at least convert to their numbers and back, which is what let the
//     weekday arithmetic drop fourteen lines of if-chain.
//   * **A `val` cannot be sliced**, third program to report that too: the two transition tables are
//     reached through a function whose whole body is an index.
//   * **A built-in is on nobody's left.** `Duration * 2` is writable and `2 * Duration` is not, so
//     the scaling in a date-time library only ever reads one way round.
//
// Deliberately not modelled, so that the program stays a guide problem rather than a standard
// library: the tz database, leap seconds, calendars other than the proleptic Gregorian, and any
// zone rule more elaborate than one yearly pair of transitions.

// The two quantities everything else is built out of, and the algebra between them.
//
// An **instant** is a point on the timeline, the same point for everybody. A **duration** is a
// length of timeline, with no place on it. The whole discipline of a date-time library is in
// keeping those two apart, because almost every operation is legal for one of them and nonsense
// for the other: two instants may be compared and subtracted but never added, while a duration may
// be added to itself, negated, and scaled.
//
// Both are counts of **microseconds**, and that choice is the one piece of representation worth
// arguing about. A `long` of nanoseconds runs out in 1678–2262, which is close enough to today to
// look fine in every test and fail on a birth date; a `long` of microseconds reaches ±292,000
// years. Nothing here needs to resolve a nanosecond, and a range that quietly ends is a worse
// defect than a precision that never begins.

const us_per_second: long = 1000000
const us_per_minute: long = 60000000
const us_per_hour: long = 3600000000
const us_per_day: long = 86400000000

// A point on the timeline, counted from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z. It names no place and no calendar:
// the same `Instant` is a different wall clock in every zone, which is the entire reason it is a
// separate type from the ones below.
struct Instant
    us: long
end Instant

// A length of timeline. It is not a number of days, because a day is not a fixed length of
// timeline — that is what `next_week` below is about.
struct Duration
    us: long
end Duration

// How far a zone's wall clock is set from UTC, in whole minutes. Minutes rather than hours because
// India is at +05:30 and Nepal at +05:45, and a library that assumes whole hours works everywhere
// its author lives.
struct Offset
    minutes: int
end Offset

seconds(n: long) -> Duration = Duration(n * us_per_second)
minutes(n: long) -> Duration = Duration(n * us_per_minute)
hours(n: long) -> Duration = Duration(n * us_per_hour)

// An instant moved by a length of time. This is the *absolute* answer — it lands exactly that many
// microseconds later on the timeline no matter what any calendar does in between.
impl Add[Duration] for Instant
    add(self, rhs: Duration) -> Instant = Instant(self.us + rhs.us)
end Instant

impl Sub[Duration] for Instant
    sub(self, rhs: Duration) -> Instant = Instant(self.us - rhs.us)
end Instant

// The difference of two instants is a duration, and **it cannot be written as `-`**. `Sub`'s method
// is `sub(self, rhs: Rhs) -> Self`, so the result is fixed to the type on the left: an
// `impl Sub[Instant] for Instant` may only produce another `Instant`, which is the one thing the
// difference of two points on a timeline is not. The operator is available and the meaning is not,
// so the most-asked-for operation in the library is a named function.
since(later: Instant, earlier: Instant) -> Duration = Duration(later.us - earlier.us)

impl Add for Duration
    add(self, rhs: Duration) -> Duration = Duration(self.us + rhs.us)
end Duration

impl Sub for Duration
    sub(self, rhs: Duration) -> Duration = Duration(self.us - rhs.us)
end Duration

impl Neg for Duration
    neg(self) -> Duration = Duration(-self.us)
end Duration

impl Mul[long] for Duration
    mul(self, rhs: long) -> Duration = Duration(self.us * rhs)
end Duration

impl Eq for Instant
    eq(self, rhs: Instant) -> bool = self.us == rhs.us
end Instant

impl Ord for Instant
    lt(self, rhs: Instant) -> bool = self.us < rhs.us
end Instant

impl Eq for Duration
    eq(self, rhs: Duration) -> bool = self.us == rhs.us
end Duration

impl Ord for Duration
    lt(self, rhs: Duration) -> bool = self.us < rhs.us
end Duration

impl Eq for Offset
    eq(self, rhs: Offset) -> bool = self.minutes == rhs.minutes
end Offset

// Whole hours of a duration, truncated toward zero, and the minutes left over. Together they are
// how every check below states a length of time, because "167 hours" is the sentence a reader can
// check against a calendar and 601200000000 is not.
whole_hours(d: Duration) -> long = d.us / us_per_hour
odd_minutes(d: Duration) -> long = d.us % us_per_hour / us_per_minute

// `+05:30`, the way an offset is written everywhere it is written down. A sign, then a width the
// reader can rely on, which is what makes a rendered timestamp sortable as text.
offset_text(o: Offset) -> string
    if o.minutes == 0 then return "Z"

    var sign = if o.minutes < 0 then "-" else "+"
    var m = if o.minutes < 0 then -o.minutes else o.minutes

    f"$sign%s${m / 60}%02d:${m % 60}%02d"
end offset_text

impl Display for Offset
    display(self, out: *Writer, fmt: FormatSpec) = display_str(offset_text(self), out, fmt)
end Offset
